# Cloudinary: direct-to-Cloudinary admin product image uploads (Phase 2,
# signed-upload flow). Required only for new image uploads from the admin
# dashboard — existing legacy_base64/static/external images keep working
# without these. CLOUDINARY_API_SECRET must never be exposed to any
# frontend (storefront or admin) — it only ever lives here, used server-side
# to sign upload params and to delete orphaned assets. CLOUDINARY_CLOUD_NAME
# is technically public-safe (Cloudinary URLs embed it) but is kept
# API-only too: every delivery URL is built server-side in
# `apps/api/src/catalog/product.mapper.ts`, so no frontend needs it either.
# CLOUDINARY_CLOUD_NAME="your-cloudinary-cloud-name"
# CLOUDINARY_API_KEY="your-cloudinary-api-key"
# CLOUDINARY_API_SECRET="your-cloudinary-api-secret"

# Video bytes are sent directly to Cloudinary; supported range >0–100 MiB.
PRODUCT_VIDEO_MAX_MB=50

# Media storage provider for NEW admin product image/video uploads.
# "cloudinary" (default — omit this var entirely for today's behavior) or
# "s3" (SeaweedFS or any S3-compatible backend). An explicit, unrecognized
# value (a typo, anything else) fails API startup rather than silently
# falling back to cloudinary. Existing cloudinary/cloudinary_video rows
# keep working and keep being read via CLOUDINARY_CLOUD_NAME regardless of
# this setting — switching providers only changes where the NEXT upload
# goes, never how past ones are read or deleted.
# MEDIA_STORAGE_PROVIDER="cloudinary"

# S3-compatible storage (required only when MEDIA_STORAGE_PROVIDER="s3" —
# validated at API startup in that case, not lazily on first upload).
#
# S3_ENDPOINT: how the API process itself reaches the S3-compatible
# gateway for HeadObject/GetObject/DeleteObject (e.g. a Docker-network
# hostname or 127.0.0.1 + port) — internal only, never given to a browser.
# S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID / S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY: credentials for that gateway.
# S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY must never be exposed to any frontend — same rule
# as CLOUDINARY_API_SECRET above.
# S3_REGION: required by the AWS SDK even though SeaweedFS ignores it —
# any non-empty value (e.g. "us-east-1") works.
# S3_MEDIA_BUCKET: the bucket product images/videos are stored in.
# S3_FORCE_PATH_STYLE: defaults to true (path-style addressing,
# bucket-name-in-URL) — SeaweedFS's S3 gateway needs this; only set to
# "false" against a backend that specifically requires virtual-hosted
# style.
# S3_UPLOAD_URL_TTL_SECONDS: how long a presigned upload URL stays valid
# (default 900 = 15 min; clamped to [60, 3600] regardless of this value).
#
# S3_PUBLIC_ENDPOINT: the public HTTPS host handed to the BROWSER for
# direct presigned-PUT uploads — must be a real, internet-reachable
# domain the admin's browser can resolve, never a Docker hostname or
# 127.0.0.1. Also doubles as the default public media READ origin (see
# S3_PUBLIC_MEDIA_BASE_URL below) in the common deployment where one
# Nginx host reverse-proxies both reads and writes to the same gateway.
# S3_PUBLIC_MEDIA_BASE_URL: set this SEPARATELY from S3_PUBLIC_ENDPOINT
# only if public reads of already-uploaded media should go through a
# different host than uploads do (e.g. a CDN in front of reads only, or
# different TLS/caching for GET vs PUT) — otherwise leave unset and it
# falls back to S3_PUBLIC_ENDPOINT. Never point either at the S3 gateway
# directly with an open/anonymous bucket policy — see the PR 4 report's
# security model.
#
# MEDIA_STORAGE_PROVIDER="s3"
# S3_ENDPOINT="http://127.0.0.1:8333"
# S3_PUBLIC_ENDPOINT="https://media.rad-object.com"
# S3_PUBLIC_MEDIA_BASE_URL=
# S3_REGION="us-east-1"
# S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID="your-access-key-id"
# S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY="your-secret-access-key"
# S3_MEDIA_BUCKET="rad-public"
# S3_FORCE_PATH_STYLE=true
# S3_UPLOAD_URL_TTL_SECONDS=900
